  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 308 PM CDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nOngoing rain showers across our southern FA are slowly waning as an \narea of low pressure well to our south continues to track east. In \nthese showers wake, stratus and drizzle will likely persist into the \nevening and overnight hours. Meanwhile up north in southern Canada, \na cold front is tracking towards the region. This cold front will \npass through slowly from north to south on Saturday. Timing of\nthe front looks to keep high temperatures near 70 degrees area \nwide Saturday afternoon, and then in its wake drop to become a \nbit cooler, in the 60s for Sunday. Temperatures will remain a\nbit cooler early in the week, as lower heights due to a weak \nupper level low pushing from the Northern Rockies late Saturday \nto the Great Lakes by early Tuesday will keep temperatures \naround or just a few degrees below average. As the week wears \non, guidance suggests that either weak upper level ridging \nbuilds in or zonal flow aloft takes control. In either scenario,\ntemperatures will be near or a few degrees above average. This \nleads to highs in the 70s most afternoons Wednesday through \nFriday. Precipitation chances will be tied to the strength of \nthe ridge and where we end up in relation to the ridge axis. If \nwe remain on the periphery of the ridge or in more zonal flow, a\nfew weak waves may track through the region bringing low \nchances for precipitation. Meanwhile, if the ridge is a little \nstronger and is more or less centered of the Red River Valley, \nprecipitation chances will remain very low.\n\n...Rain Today, Fog/Drizzle Tonight...\n\nRain is slowly ending from west to east across southern reaches of \nthe FA. In its wake, several observation sites are reporting \ndrizzle. Soundings show saturation up to 2 Km in depth persisting \ninto tonight with sufficient low level lift. Therefore, patches\nof drizzle and fog will remain a concern into Saturday morning.\nIt is uncertain when stratus and drizzle will burn off Saturday\nmorning, as for the most persistent locations (which are\ncurrently favored to be in far southeastern ND), it may take \nuntil the drier air behind the cold front comes through during \nthe afternoon hours.",
